# Vector C2 Configuration Template
# Copy to .env for local setup. DO NOT commit .env to Git.
# See SECURITY.md for the threat model these settings affect.

# ---- Backend node ----
PORT=8080
NODE_ID=vector-node-home-nas

# Serve wss:// instead of plaintext ws://. Message bodies are end-to-end
# encrypted either way, but without TLS the routing metadata (who is talking to
# whom, and when) is exposed to anything on the network path.
#
# Prefer the compose overlay over setting these by hand — it also fixes the
# healthcheck, which otherwise probes http:// and restart-loops a healthy node:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.tls.yml up -d
# For an isolated network, generate a CA first:
#   backend/scripts/make-internal-ca.sh ./certs nas.local 192.168.1.20
# Full walkthrough in DEPLOYMENT.md.
# TLS_CERT_FILE=/certs/node.crt
# TLS_KEY_FILE=/certs/node.key

# Browser origins allowed to open WebSockets. Leave unset for native clients:
# they send no Origin header and are unaffected, while a hostile web page is
# refused. Comma-separated.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://c2.example.com

# ---- Client seed nodes ----
# Passed at build time, not read from this file at runtime:
#   flutter build apk --dart-define=CLOUD_MESH_NODE_URL=https://c2.example.com
# A build with no defines only tries localhost. Prefer https:// so the client
# negotiates wss://.
CLOUD_MESH_NODE_URL=https://c2.example.com
NAS_MESH_NODE_URL=https://nas.local:8443
LOCAL_MESH_NODE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
FIELD_ROUTER_NODE_URL=https://field-router.local:8443

# ---- Client transport strictness ----
# Also a --dart-define, not read at runtime.
#
#   private   (default) https:// anywhere; plaintext only to loopback and
#             private-network addresses. A routable plaintext node is refused
#             and the refusal is written to the operator's event log.
#   tls-only  https:// only, including on the LAN.
#   any       plaintext to anything. Needed only for split-horizon DNS, where a
#             public-looking name resolves to a LAN address.
TRANSPORT_POLICY=private

# Base64 PEM of a CA to trust for relay certificates, in addition to the platform
# roots. Required for wss:// on an isolated network, which cannot obtain a
# publicly-trusted certificate. Leave unset for a public node.
#   --dart-define=RELAY_CA_PEM_BASE64="$(base64 < backend/certs/ca.crt | tr -d '\n')"
# RELAY_CA_PEM_BASE64=

# ---- Call path (WebRTC ICE) ----
# Also --dart-defines. Media is DTLS-SRTP peer-to-peer and never touches the
# relay; these only affect how a path between the two devices is found.
#
# STUN discovers this device's public address. The default is Google's public
# servers, which means placing a call discloses this device's IP to Google — the
# app says so in the event log. Set to empty on an isolated network: the public
# servers are unreachable there anyway and host candidates carry the call.
STUN_SERVERS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302,stun:stun1.l.google.com:19302

# TURN relays media when no direct path exists. Empty by default, deliberately:
# a call that cannot find a direct path fails visibly rather than quietly routing
# through a third party. A TURN relay sees both endpoints' addresses and the
# encrypted stream — it cannot listen to the call, but it learns who called whom,
# when, and from where. Enabling it is logged for the operator. See SECURITY.md.
# TURN_URLS=turn:relay.example.com:3478
# TURN_USERNAME=
# TURN_CREDENTIAL=
